const LAWX = {
  asof: '2026-11-19',
  items: [
    /* ---------- the license ---------- */
    { id: 'c39', grp: 'License', t: 'A contractor license for any job of $1,000 or more: roofing is class C-39', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7026, 7048 (Stats. 2024, ch. 240, AB 2622; Stats. 2025, ch. 67, AB 1170); Cal. Code Regs. tit. 16, 832.39', eff: '$1,000 line from January 1, 2025 (it was $500)', g: 'A', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['BPC 7048'],
      s: `Anyone who offers, bids or does work to build, alter, repair or improve a structure is a contractor, subcontractors included. The only exemption is minor work: the total price for labor, materials and everything else is under $1,000, and the work needs no building permit, and the person employs no one. Splitting a job into smaller contracts to stay under the line is not allowed. The roofing class is C-39: it covers installing and repairing surfaces that seal, waterproof and weatherproof structures, from shingles, tile, slate and shakes to metal roofing systems, urethane foam and membranes. In practice almost every reroof needs a licensed contractor, because the price is over $1,000 and most cities require a permit (that last sentence is our reading, grade D). What a roofer must do: hold an active C-39 (or another class that covers the work) before offering or bidding any roofing job.` },
    { id: 'licnum', grp: 'License', t: 'The license number goes in every ad, contract and bid', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7030.5; 7027.1 and 7027.2 (Stats. 2024, ch. 240, AB 2622)', eff: 'In force; 7027.2 as amended January 1, 2025', g: 'B', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['BPC 7030.5'],
      s: `Every licensee must include the license number in all construction contracts, in subcontracts and calls for bid, and in all forms of advertising, as the registrar prescribes. Advertising as a contractor without a license is a misdemeanor with a fine of $700 to $1,000 on top of any other punishment. An unlicensed person may advertise only for jobs under $1,000 and only if the ad says the person is not licensed. What a roofer must do: print the CSLB license number on every ad, page, truck, sign, door hanger, estimate and contract. Section 7030.5 was read on a transcription host, so it is graded B; sections 7027.1 and 7027.2 were read as archive captures of the Legislature's pages.` },
    { id: 'bond', grp: 'License', t: 'A $25,000 contractor bond on file', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7071.6 (Stats. 2021, ch. 367, SB 607), 7071.9; CSLB, Bond Requirements', eff: 'Operative January 1, 2023', g: 'A', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['BPC 7071.6'],
      s: `Every applicant and licensee keeps a contractor's bond of $25,000 on file with the Contractors State License Board. Claimants other than homeowners and employees are capped at $7,500 in aggregate; the rest is reserved for the homeowner and employee beneficiaries. A license qualified by a responsible managing employee, or by an officer who owns less than 10 percent, also needs a $25,000 bond of qualifying individual. After a revocation the board may require a disciplinary bond of at least $25,000 and up to ten times the contractor's bond for at least two years. No general liability minimum was found in the sections read; a home improvement contract must instead say whether the contractor carries commercial general liability insurance.` },
    { id: 'wc', grp: 'License', t: 'Workers\' compensation for every C-39 roofer, employees or not', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7125, 7125.4 (SB 291), 7126; Insurance Code 11665; Labor Code 3800', eff: 'Roofer rule since January 1, 2013 (AB 2219); higher penalties from 2026; all licensees from January 1, 2028', g: 'B', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['BPC 7125'],
      s: `Other contractors with no employees may file an exemption. A C-39 licensee may not: a certificate of workers' compensation insurance must be on file with the board even with no employees. The same now applies to C-8 concrete, C-20 heating and air conditioning, C-22 asbestos abatement and D-49 tree service, and the current section sunsets on January 1, 2028, the date the rule is due to reach every licensee. The workers' compensation insurer must audit each C-39 policyholder's payroll every year with a visit in person. Cities and counties check coverage when a permit issues. From 2026 the minimum civil penalty for employing workers without coverage is $10,000 per violation for a sole owner and $20,000 for a partnership, corporation or limited liability company, up to $30,000 per occurrence for repeats; a violation is also a misdemeanor. That Senate Bill 1455 of 2024 set the 2028 date was seen only in a search summary.` },
    { id: 'his', grp: 'License', t: 'Anyone who sells roofs at the home registers as a home improvement salesperson', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7152, 7153, 7153.3, 7154; CSLB, Home Improvement Salesperson registration', eff: 'In force (7152 as updated January 1, 2026)', g: 'A', lines: LX_RES, url: LAWLINK['CSLB home improvement salesperson'],
      s: `A person who solicits, sells, negotiates or signs home improvement contracts for a licensed contractor must be registered with the board, whatever the dollar amount. Exceptions: officers of record, general partners, the qualifier, sales in a store that the buyer started, people who only set appointments for a registered salesperson, and service staff doing only the repair the buyer asked for. Selling without a current registration is a misdemeanor. The contractor must tell the board in writing before the salesperson starts and within 90 days after they leave. Before any sales activity the salesperson must give the owner or tenant the contractor's business name and license number. A registration runs two years. The registration fee was not found in the sources opened.` },
    { id: 'unlic', grp: 'License', t: 'Unlicensed contracting: fines, jail and no right to be paid', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7028, 7028.7 (SB 779), 7031, 7099.2', eff: 'In force; higher minimum civil penalties from July 1, 2026 by the CSLB bulletin', g: 'A', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['BPC 7028'],
      s: `A first conviction is a misdemeanor: a fine up to $5,000, up to six months in county jail, or both. A second carries a fine of 20 percent of the contract price or $5,000, whichever is greater, and at least 90 days in jail. The board may also cite an unlicensed person for a civil penalty of $1,500 to $15,000. An unlicensed contractor cannot sue to be paid, and the customer may sue to take back everything paid, even for work done. Licensees face civil penalties of $500 to $8,000 in general and $1,500 to $30,000 for the most serious violations. The exact effective date of the new minimums rests on the CSLB bulletin (July 1, 2026).` },
    { id: 'disasterlic', grp: 'Disaster', t: 'Unlicensed work on disaster damage can be charged as a felony', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7028.16 (Stats. 2020, ch. 364, SB 1189); Penal Code 667.16', eff: 'In force since January 1, 2021', g: 'A', lines: ['fire', 'storm', 'repair', 'replace', 'tile', 'mh'], url: LAWLINK['BPC 7028.16'],
      s: `Contracting without a license to repair damage from a natural disaster for which the Governor has proclaimed a state of emergency, or the President has declared an emergency or major disaster, may be punished as a felony: a fine up to $10,000, or 16 months, two years or three years, or both; or as a misdemeanor with a fine up to $1,000 or up to one year in county jail. A felony theft or forgery conviction in a scheme to defraud an owner over disaster repairs carries one extra year. What a roofer must do: crews brought into a burn area or a storm area work only under an active California license.` },
    /* ---------- the home improvement contract ---------- */
    { id: 'hic', grp: 'Contract', t: 'A written home improvement contract for any residential job over $500', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7151, 7159 (Stats. 2020, ch. 312, SB 1474; 2026 text with AB 1327)', eff: 'In force', g: 'A', lines: LX_RES, url: LAWLINK['BPC 7159 (2021 text)'],
      s: `Home improvement means repairing, remodeling, altering or modernizing residential property, and rebuilding a home damaged or destroyed by a disaster with a proclaimed state of emergency. Reroofing is home improvement. When the total price is over $500 the contract must be in writing, signed and dated by both sides, and a copy given to the buyer before any work starts. It must carry the contractor's name, business address and license number; the salesperson's name and registration number; the heading "Home Improvement" in at least 10 point bold; the contract price; any finance charge shown separately; a description of the project and the significant materials; the down payment; a schedule of progress payments; approximate start and completion dates; and a note on extra work and change orders. Required notices cover commercial general liability insurance, workers' compensation, the Mechanics Lien Warning, information about the CSLB, the right to cancel, and the right to require a performance and payment bond. Text is at least 10 point, the main warnings 12 point bold. A change order is in writing and signed before the changed work starts.` },
    { id: 'downpay', grp: 'Contract', t: 'Down payment: $1,000 or 10 percent, whichever is less', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7159.5 (Stats. 2023, ch. 403, SB 601); 7159(d)(8)(C), (d)(9)(C)', eff: 'In force', g: 'A', lines: LX_RES, url: LAWLINK['BPC 7159.5'],
      s: `A down payment on a home improvement contract may not exceed $1,000 or 10 percent of the contract price, whichever amount is less, and the contract must print that sentence. Apart from the down payment a contractor may neither ask for nor accept payment that exceeds the value of the work performed or the material delivered. Breaking the price, down payment or advance payment rules is a misdemeanor: a fine of $100 to $5,000, up to one year in county jail, or both; in an area damaged by a disaster with a proclaimed emergency the court must impose the maximum fine. On request the contractor gives an unconditional lien release for the part already paid, and the owner may hold further payments until it comes. A contractor who furnishes a performance and payment bond, or a blanket bond or joint control the registrar has approved, is exempt from the down payment and progress payment limits.` },
    { id: 'cancel', grp: 'Contract', t: 'Right to cancel: three business days, five for a senior, seven after a disaster', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7159(e)(6), (e)(7), (f); Civil Code 1689.6', eff: 'Five day rule for contracts from January 1, 2021', g: 'A', lines: LX_RES, url: LAWLINK['BPC 7159 (2021 text)'],
      s: `The buyer may cancel a home improvement contract until midnight of the third business day after receiving a signed and dated copy that includes the notice. For a buyer who is a senior citizen the notice says five and fifth. A contract written to repair or restore a home damaged by a sudden or catastrophic event with a federal, state or local emergency declaration carries a notice titled "Seven-Day Right to Cancel". The buyer cancels by emailing, mailing, faxing or delivering a written notice to the contractor's place of business, and the contractor returns anything paid within 10 days of receiving it. The notice is not required for a contract negotiated at the contractor's place of business. The age that makes a buyer a senior citizen for this rule was not read in the sections opened, and neither was the Civil Code's definition of a business day: confirm both before relying on a date.` },
    { id: 'emailcancel', grp: 'Contract', t: 'From 2026: cancellation by email, and subcontractors named on request', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7159 (AB 1327, Aguiar-Curry); SB 517 (Niello); CSLB Industry Bulletin 25-04', eff: 'January 1, 2026', g: 'A', lines: LX_RES, url: LAWLINK['CSLB bulletin 25-04 (new laws for 2026)'],
      s: `The first page of a home improvement contract must now carry the name, address and email address of the contractor to whom the Notice of Cancellation is sent, and a telephone number that helps the buyer find and fill in the notice. Buyers may cancel by email. A contractor who uses subcontractors on a home improvement project must, on request, give each subcontractor's name, contact details, license number and classification. What a roofer must do: update every contract form and watch the cancellation inbox every day.` },
    { id: 'svcrepair', grp: 'Contract', t: 'Service and repair contracts: the short form stops at $750', cite: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7159.10, 7159.14; Civil Code 1689.6(a)(3)', eff: 'In force', g: 'B', lines: ['repair', 'storm', 'mh'], url: LAWLINK['BPC 7159.10'],
      s: `The short service and repair form is allowed only when the price is $750 or less, the buyer made the first contact, the contractor sells nothing beyond what the problem needs, and no payment is due or taken until the work is complete. Otherwise the full home improvement contract rules apply at any price. The buyer may cancel until receiving the signed and dated copy and the work starts. Violations in a disaster area with a proclaimed emergency can add a fine of $500 to $25,000. Most leak repairs priced above $750 therefore need the full contract.` },
    { id: 'warranty', grp: 'Contract', t: 'Roof warranties: in writing, transferable, and "lifetime" must say whose', cite: 'Civil Code 1797.91, 1797.92, 1797.93, 1797.94', eff: 'Contracts from January 1, 1994', g: 'B', lines: LX_RES, url: LAWLINK['Civil Code 1797.91'],
      s: `A contract for roofing materials, or to install, repair or replace all or part of the roof of a residential structure, must be in writing if it includes any warranty that runs past completion. The warranty passes to every later buyer of the home unless a clear and conspicuous clause limits transfer; that limit must sit in a box at the top of the first page of the warranty in 14 point bold. A warranty that uses "lifetime", "life" or a similar word must disclose, clearly enough to be noticed and understood, the life it refers to.` },
    { id: 'fireclass', grp: 'Fire code', t: 'The installer certifies the roof\'s fire class to the owner', cite: 'Health & Safety Code 13132.7(i)', eff: 'In force', g: 'B', lines: ['replace', 'harden', 'fire', 'tile', 'metal', 'mf', 'comm', 'newc'], url: LAWLINK['Health and Safety Code 13132.7'],
      s: `The installer of a roof covering must give the building owner the certification of the roof covering classification, as the manufacturer or supplier provides it, and give it to the building department on request. The installer must also install the covering in accordance with the manufacturer's listing. What a roofer must do: hand over the classification sheet with the final invoice and keep a copy in the job file.` },
    /* ---------- liens ---------- */
    { id: 'lien', grp: 'Liens', t: 'Mechanics liens: preliminary notice in 20 days, record in 90', cite: 'Civil Code 8200, 8204, 8412, 8414, 8416, 8460; Bus. & Prof. Code 7159(e)(4), 7159.5(a)(6)', eff: 'In force (operative July 1, 2012)', g: 'A', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['Civil Code 8200'],
      s: `Subcontractors and suppliers must give a preliminary notice to the owner, the direct contractor and any construction lender no later than 20 days after first furnishing work; a late notice protects only work from 20 days before it was served. A roofer hired directly by the owner gives the notice only to a construction lender, if there is one. Without the notice there is no valid lien. A direct contractor records the lien after completing the contract and before the earlier of 90 days after completion of the work or 60 days after the owner records a notice of completion or cessation; subcontractors and suppliers have the earlier of 90 days or 30 days after that recorded notice. A copy with the Notice of Mechanics Lien is served on the owner. Suit to enforce must start within 90 days of recording or the lien expires. Every home improvement contract tells the owner, in the Mechanics Lien Warning, that unpaid subcontractors and suppliers can lien the home even if the contractor was paid.` },
    /* ---------- declared disasters ---------- */
    { id: 'gouge', grp: 'Disaster', t: 'Price gouging: no more than 10 percent above the pre emergency price, for 180 days', cite: 'Penal Code 396(b), (c), (g), (h)', eff: 'On any federal, state or local emergency declaration', g: 'B', lines: ['fire', 'storm', 'repair', 'replace', 'tile', 'harden', 'mh', 'mf', 'comm'], url: LAWLINK['Penal Code 396'],
      s: `For 180 days after an emergency is proclaimed or declared, a contractor may not sell or offer repair or reconstruction services, or emergency cleanup services, for more than 10 percent above the price that contractor charged immediately before the declaration. Building materials and other goods carry the same 10 percent cap for 30 days. A larger increase is lawful only if the contractor can prove it was directly attributable to higher labor or material costs, and the price is no more than 10 percent above the contractor's cost plus the markup the contractor customarily applies. The Governor, the Legislature or a local body can extend the periods; each local extension is at most 30 days. A violation is a misdemeanor: up to one year in county jail, a fine up to $10,000, or both, and it is also unfair competition. What a roofer must do: keep a dated price book from before each declaration and the supplier invoices behind any increase.` },
    /* ---------- insurance claims ---------- */
    { id: 'padj', grp: 'Claims', t: 'Negotiating a claim for pay needs a public adjuster license', cite: 'Insurance Code 15006, 15007; People v. Montgomery-Sansome, LP (San Mateo County Superior Court No. 17CIV04659)', eff: 'In force', g: 'A', lines: ['storm', 'fire', 'repair', 'replace', 'tile', 'mf', 'comm'], url: LAWLINK['Insurance Code 15006'],
      s: `A public insurance adjuster is a person who, for compensation, acts for or aids an insured in negotiating or settling a property claim, or who advertises or holds out as an adjuster of those claims. Doing that without the license carries a civil penalty up to $10,000, or $25,000 if willful. A San Mateo County trial court applied the Act to a restoration contractor that offered to negotiate with insurers at no cost in return for the repair job: $1,250 per violation, $56,250 in total, and an injunction against negotiating or advocating for a homeowner with an insurer. That is one trial court decision, read on a law firm's site (grade C); it does not bind other courts and an appeal was not checked. What a contractor may safely do, by our reading only (grade D, no statute or agency notice draws the line): write an estimate and scope of work, give it to the owner, and meet the adjuster to explain the construction facts.` },
    { id: 'deductible', grp: 'Claims', t: 'No discount meant to offset a deductible, and no referral money to agents or adjusters', cite: 'Penal Code 551(a), (b), (c)', eff: 'In force', g: 'B', lines: ['storm', 'fire', 'repair', 'replace', 'tile', 'metal', 'mf', 'comm'], url: LAWLINK['Penal Code 551'],
      s: `It is unlawful for a contractor, or its employees or agents, to knowingly offer or give any discount intended to offset a deductible required by a policy covering repairs to a residential or commercial structure. The one exception: the insurer has determined the amount of the claim and the work is done in accordance with that determination or with estimates the insurer accepted. Advertising a discount is allowed only on the same condition. It is also unlawful to offer an insurance agent, broker or adjuster any fee, commission or other consideration for referring an insured. Where more than $950 is at issue the offense is punishable by 16 months, two years or three years, a fine up to $10,000, or both, or by up to one year in county jail; otherwise by up to six months, $1,000, or both. Unlike some states, no bold contract notice about the deductible was found in California law.` },
    { id: 'aob', grp: 'Claims', t: 'Assignment of benefits: no roofing statute found', cite: 'Insurance Code 520', eff: 'In force', g: 'D', lines: ['storm', 'fire', 'mf', 'comm'], url: LAWLINK['Insurance Code 520'],
      s: `The search found no California statute specific to roofing or repair assignments. That is an absence in the search, not proof there is none. The general rule read is Insurance Code 520: a policy clause that forbids transferring a claim after the loss is void if made before the loss. An assignment does not make claim negotiation by an unlicensed person lawful (see the public adjuster row).` },
    { id: 'padjrules', grp: 'Claims', t: 'Public adjusters and contractors stay apart', cite: 'Insurance Code 15027, 15027.5; Department of Insurance, "After the Fires" flyer (January 2025)', eff: 'In force', g: 'A', lines: ['fire', 'storm', 'mf', 'comm'], url: LAWLINK['Department of Insurance, After the Fires flyer'],
      s: `A public adjuster's contract is in writing on a form the Commissioner approves, and the insured may cancel within three business days. A public adjuster may not have an interest in any contractor or any company repairing the property, may not offer referral fees above $100, and must disclose to the insured any compensation from a contractor or vendor connected to the claim. Public adjusters may not solicit while the loss is under way, for seven days after a declared disaster area reopens, or between 6 p.m. and 8 a.m. The Department tells owners not to sign any repair contract until they have spoken to their insurer. A 2025 bill to cap public adjuster fees at 15 percent (AB 597) was seen only in a search summary as held in committee.` },
    { id: 'claimclock', grp: 'Claims', t: 'The insurer\'s clock: 15, 40 and 30 days', cite: 'Cal. Code Regs. tit. 10, 2695.5(e), 2695.7(b), (c)(1), (h) (Fair Claims Settlement Practices Regulations)', eff: 'In force', g: 'B', lines: ['storm', 'fire', 'repair', 'replace', 'tile', 'mf', 'comm'], url: LAWLINK['10 CCR 2695.7'],
      s: `On notice of a claim the insurer must, within 15 calendar days, acknowledge it, provide forms, instructions and reasonable assistance, and begin any necessary investigation. Within 40 calendar days of receiving proof of claim it must accept or deny the claim in whole or in part; if it needs more time it says so in writing within the 40 days and again every 30 calendar days. Within 30 calendar days of accepting it must tender payment. A denial is in writing with every factual and legal reason. No fixed statutory penalty percentage was found. The clock is for planning cash flow on insurance paid jobs and for explaining the timeline in general terms.` },
    { id: 'matching', grp: 'Claims', t: 'Matching, labor not depreciated, and no steering to a roofer', cite: 'Cal. Code Regs. tit. 10, 2695.9(a)(1), (a)(2), (b), (c), (d), (f); Health & Safety Code 13132.7(k)', eff: 'In force (labor rule operative August 30, 2006)', g: 'B', lines: ['storm', 'fire', 'repair', 'replace', 'tile', 'mf', 'comm'], url: LAWLINK['10 CCR 2695.9'],
      s: `On a replacement cost policy, when replaced items do not match in quality, color or size, the insurer replaces all items in the damaged area so that it has a reasonably uniform appearance. Consequential damage from making the repair is part of the loss, and the insured pays only the deductible. The expense of labor to repair, rebuild or replace is not a component of physical depreciation; only materials normally replaced during a building's life are depreciated, and each adjustment must be itemized in dollars. The insurer gives the claimant a copy of its estimate, which must reflect costs in the local market; if the owner's contractor's estimate is higher the insurer pays the difference or, on request, names a repairer who will do the work for its figure. An insurer may not require a particular repairer, and may recommend one only when asked or after telling the owner in writing of the right to choose. Fire retardant wood roofing used to patch untreated wood roofing satisfies the matching rule.` },
    { id: 'rcv', grp: 'Claims', t: 'Replacement cost: 12 months to finish and collect, 36 after a declared emergency', cite: 'Insurance Code 2051, 2051.5, 2071', eff: 'In force; policy forms must comply with 2051.5 from July 1, 2026', g: 'B', lines: ['storm', 'fire', 'repair', 'replace', 'tile', 'mf', 'comm'], url: LAWLINK['Insurance Code 2051.5'],
      s: `Actual cash value is the cost to repair, rebuild or replace less fair physical depreciation, and depreciation applies only to components normally replaced during the structure's life. On a replacement cost policy the insurer pays actual cash value first and the difference once the property is repaired or replaced. The policy may not give the insured less than 12 months from the first actual cash value payment to collect full replacement cost, or less than 36 months for a loss in a declared state of emergency, with six month extensions for good cause; good cause includes permit delays, missing materials and the unavailability of contractors. After a declared emergency an insurer may not require proof of loss sooner than 100 days after the loss. Suit against the insurer must start within 12 months of the start of the loss, 24 months for a declared emergency. Which 2025 act added the 100 day rule was not identified.` },
    /* ---------- insurance market ---------- */
    { id: 'nonrenew', grp: 'Insurance market', t: 'Non-renewal: 75 days notice, and a one year moratorium around a wildfire', cite: 'Insurance Code 678 (SB 1242), 675.1 (SB 894; SB 824 of 2018)', eff: 'In force', g: 'A', lines: ['harden', 'fire', 'replace', 'tile', 'metal'], url: LAWLINK['Insurance Code 675.1'],
      s: `An insurer must deliver a notice of non-renewal at least 75 days before the policy expires. After a total loss in a disaster the insurer must offer to renew for at least the next two annual periods, and no less than 24 months from the loss, and may not cancel while the home is being rebuilt. For one year after the Governor declares a state of emergency, an insurer may not cancel or refuse to renew a residential policy in any ZIP Code within or adjacent to the fire perimeter solely because the home is in an area where a wildfire has occurred; the Commissioner names the ZIP Codes in a bulletin. Declarations listed by the Department on November 19, 2026 include the Dove Fire (November 9, 2026) and the Gann Fire (August 6, 2026), and for 2025 the Gifford, Pack, TCU September Lightning Complex, Franklin and Hughes fires and the January 7, 2025 declaration for the Palisades, Eaton, Hurst, Lidia, Sunset, Woodley and Olivas fires. The ZIP Code lists could not be downloaded. By arithmetic the January 2025 moratorium ended in January 2026 unless extended; no extension was found (grade D).` },
    { id: 'fairplan', grp: 'Insurance market', t: 'The FAIR Plan: insurer of last resort, with wildfire hardening discounts', cite: 'California FAIR Plan Association, Key Statistics (September 2026) and Wildfire Hardening Discounts flyer (policies effective November 15, 2025 or later)', eff: 'Figures at September 30, 2026', g: 'A', lines: ['harden', 'fire', 'replace', 'tile', 'metal', 'mf', 'comm'], url: LAWLINK['FAIR Plan wildfire hardening discounts'],
      s: `The FAIR Plan had 698,307 policies in force at September 2026, up 157 percent since September 2022, with $773 billion of exposure. Its dwelling policy is a named peril policy; owners buy a separate Difference in Conditions policy for wider cover, so a wind or rain leak claim on a home insured only by the FAIR Plan depends on that second policy (our reading, grade D). Policyholders may qualify for up to 12 discounts on the wildfire part of the premium: all 12 give up to 16.4 percent off that part on a dwelling policy and up to 13.8 percent on a commercial policy. A Class A fire rated roof is one of five structure discounts, with enclosed eaves, ember and fire resistant vents, multi pane windows or shutters, and six inches of noncombustible material at the base of the walls. The percentage for the roof alone is not published in the flyer.` },
    { id: 'sfw', grp: 'Insurance market', t: 'Safer from Wildfires: insurers must reflect a Class A roof in the rate, at no set percentage', cite: 'Cal. Code Regs. tit. 10, 2644.9; Department of Insurance, FAQ: Safer from Wildfires Regulation (September 2026)', eff: 'Operative October 14, 2022', g: 'B', lines: ['harden', 'fire', 'replace', 'tile', 'metal'], url: LAWLINK['10 CCR 2644.9'],
      s: `Any insurer that prices on wildfire risk must take into account and reflect each listed mitigation in its rating plan. The five building items are a Class A fire rated roof, enclosed eaves, fire resistant vents, multi pane windows or working shutters, and at least six inches of noncombustible vertical clearance at the bottom of the exterior walls. Five items cover the surroundings, and two cover the community (a Fire Risk Reduction Community, or a Firewise USA site in good standing). The regulation sets no percentage: the Department says discounts depend on the property and that different insurers may offer different discounts. Most roofs qualify as Class A, including asphalt shingles, concrete, brick or masonry tiles and metal; wood shake does not. The saving shows at the start of the next policy period after the owner reports the work, and proof or an inspection may be needed. Insurers must give the wildfire risk score in writing and allow an appeal. Roof discount percentages by insurer were not found.` },
    /* ---------- building code ---------- */
    { id: 'permit', grp: 'Building code', t: 'Reroof permits depend on the city', cite: 'City of Los Angeles permit data; San Diego Information Bulletin 123; Los Angeles County Building Code Manual 1512 Article 1; San Francisco permit data; Labor Code 3800', eff: 'As read November 19, 2026', g: 'A', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['San Diego Information Bulletin 123'],
      s: `There is no single state reroof permit statute in the sources read; each city or county decides. City of Los Angeles: permits are issued for reroofs in very large numbers on a standard description (11,954 in 2025); the municipal code sentence that requires them was not opened. City of San Diego: no building permit to renew a roof covering where the roof structure is not altered; a permit is needed if framing or sheathing is altered, if shakes or shingles give way to material heavier than 6 pounds per square foot, if skip sheathing is replaced, or on a historic property, and up to 25 percent of the deck may be replaced in kind without one on houses, duplexes and townhouses. Los Angeles County (unincorporated): a building permit for the reroof, plus a separate solar permit if panels come off and go back. San Francisco: reroof permits are filed over the counter (4,084 in 2025). San Jose and Sacramento were not opened. Permit counts therefore measure the reroof market only where a permit is required.` },
    { id: 'reroof', grp: 'Building code', t: 'Tear off, recover and relaying tile', cite: 'California Residential Code R908.3, R908.3.1.1, R908.4, R908.5 (2022 and 2025); California Building Code 1505.1.2 (2022)', eff: '2025 edition in force January 1, 2026', g: 'B', lines: ['replace', 'repair', 'tile', 'metal', 'storm', 'fire', 'mf', 'comm'], url: LAWLINK['2025 Residential Code, chapter 9'],
      s: `A roof replacement removes every existing layer down to the deck. A second layer is not allowed where the roof is water soaked or deteriorated, where the existing covering is slate, clay, cement or asbestos cement tile, or where there are already two or more layers. Sound slate, clay or cement tile may be reinstalled; damaged, cracked or broken tile may not, and rusted or damaged flashings, edgings and vents are replaced. That sentence is the legal basis for a tile lift and relay. Tile or metal over wood shakes needs a fire barrier layer first. The 2025 code lets existing self adhered underlayment stay under conditions. Outside fire zones the statewide floor is Class C, for new roofs, for the whole roof when more than 50 percent is replaced in a year, and for any repaired part. Code text was read on UpCodes, not the official viewer.` },
    { id: 'codecycle', grp: 'Building code', t: 'The 2025 codes are in force, and residential rules are frozen to mid 2031', cite: 'California Building Standards Commission, Codes page and Information Bulletin 25-03; AB 130 (Stats. 2025, ch. 22)', eff: '2025 Title 24 from January 1, 2026; freeze October 1, 2025 to June 1, 2031', g: 'A', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['Building Standards Commission bulletin 25-03'],
      s: `The 2025 California Building Standards Code (Title 24) took effect January 1, 2026; the 2022 edition had applied since January 1, 2023. The 2025 Energy Code applies to permit applications filed on or after January 1, 2026. Assembly Bill 130 stops new state and local building standards for residential units from October 1, 2025 to June 1, 2031. The exceptions: rules already in effect on September 30, 2025, emergency health and safety standards, and home hardening, which the Commission reads as local amendments to the Wildland-Urban Interface Code. The 2025 residential rules in this register are therefore likely to stand until mid 2031, apart from wildfire rules (our reading, grade D).` },
    /* ---------- fire code ---------- */
    { id: 'classa', grp: 'Fire code', t: 'Class A roof in a fire zone when 50 percent or more is replaced in 12 months', cite: '2025 California Wildland-Urban Interface Code (Title 24, Part 7) 101.3.1, 504.2, 507.1; 2022 California Building Code 705A.1, 1505.1.1', eff: 'January 1, 2026 (the 2022 code said "more than 50 percent")', g: 'B', lines: ['replace', 'harden', 'fire', 'tile', 'metal', 'repair', 'storm', 'mf', 'comm', 'newc'], url: LAWLINK['2025 Wildland-Urban Interface Code, chapter 5'],
      s: `The 2025 edition moved the wildfire construction rules from Chapter 7A of the Building Code into a new Wildland-Urban Interface Code. It applies on all State Responsibility Area land, in High and Very High Fire Hazard Severity Zones in Local Responsibility Areas, and in local interface areas. On a building that existed before the code, a roof covering that is replaced, or has 50 percent or more replaced in a 12 month period, must be replaced with the covering required for new construction, and every part applied in an addition, alteration or repair must be at least Class A. The roof must be a Class A assembly when tested to ASTM E108 or UL 790. Class A is the rating of an assembly (covering, underlayment and deck together), not of a shingle or a tile on its own. Some assemblies count as Class A without a test, such as clay or concrete tile, metal or slate on a noncombustible deck. Under the 2022 code the trigger was more than 50 percent within one year, in any Fire Hazard Severity Zone. A jurisdiction that adopts the State Fire Marshal's model ordinance is outside the state sentence (2022 text).` },
    { id: 'wuidetail', grp: 'Fire code', t: 'What a fire zone roof needs besides the covering', cite: '2025 California Wildland-Urban Interface Code 503.2.3, 504.2.1.1, 504.2.1.2, 504.2.2, 504.3, 504.4, 504.10; 2022 California Building Code 705A.2 to 705A.4, 706A.2', eff: 'Roofing and vent rules from December 1, 2005; full chapter for permits from July 1, 2008', g: 'B', lines: ['harden', 'replace', 'tile', 'fire', 'metal', 'newc', 'mf'], url: LAWLINK['2025 Wildland-Urban Interface Code, chapter 5'],
      s: `Where tile or another profile leaves an air space over a combustible deck, a 72 pound mineral surfaced cap sheet to ASTM D3909 goes over the deck (or a Class A rated underlayment, or one inch of mineral wool board). Bird stops close the eaves where the profile fits, and hip and ridge caps are mudded in. Valleys take metal flashing of at least 0.019 inch over a 36 inch wide cap sheet for the full length. Gutters need a means to keep leaves and debris out, and the 2025 code adds that gutters and downspouts are noncombustible. Vents are listed flame and ember resistant vents, or carry noncombustible corrosion resistant mesh between 1/16 and 1/8 inch. Enclosed eaves and soffits are protected with noncombustible or ignition resistant material. Fire retardant treated wood shakes must form a Class A assembly. Buildings built before July 1, 2008 are otherwise outside the chapter for additions and remodels; the reroof rule is the exception that reaches them.` },
    { id: 'fhsz', grp: 'Fire code', t: 'Fire Hazard Severity Zones and the 2025 maps', cite: '2025 California Wildland-Urban Interface Code 101.3.1; State Fire Marshal maps (not opened)', eff: 'New local area maps reported in early 2025', g: 'D', lines: ['harden', 'replace', 'fire', 'tile', 'metal', 'newc'], url: LAWLINK['2025 Wildland-Urban Interface Code, chapter 1'],
      s: `The code text on where the rules apply was read (see the Class A row). The maps were not: the State Fire Marshal's pages refused automated reading. City pages seen only in search summaries say the State Fire Marshal issued new Local Responsibility Area maps in early 2025 that show Moderate and High zones as well as Very High for the first time, and one says about 1.4 million acres were added to High and Very High. A 2019 news report quotes CAL FIRE that as many as 3 million homes lie in the zones. Treat the acreage, the dates and the home count as leads, not facts, and check an address on the State Fire Marshal's viewer before quoting a fire zone roof.` },
    { id: 'shake', grp: 'Fire code', t: 'Wood shakes: listed products only statewide, banned outright in Los Angeles', cite: 'Health & Safety Code 13132.7(b), (d), (f), (j); Los Angeles Department of Building and Safety Information Bulletin P/BC 2026-013; Los Angeles Ordinance 165047; San Diego Information Bulletin 123', eff: 'Los Angeles since August 25, 1989; bulletin effective January 1, 2026', g: 'A', lines: ['replace', 'repair', 'harden', 'fire'], url: LAWLINK['Los Angeles wood shake bulletin'],
      s: `No wood roof covering may be sold or applied in California unless the State Fire Marshal has approved and listed it and it has passed at least 5 years of the 10 year natural weathering test. The statute's floor is Class A in Very High zones, Class B in State Responsibility Areas other than Moderate, and Class C everywhere else; the building codes now go further in fire zones. Cities may be stricter. In the City of Los Angeles all wood shake and wood shingle roof covering is prohibited for new work, reroofing and repairs; only a minor repair up to 5 percent may be allowed on request, and reroofing more than 50 percent in a year means all remaining shakes come off. The City of San Diego permits no wood shingle or shake renewals and no new covering over existing wood. A 2003 Orange County grand jury report lists other bans (Santa Barbara County, the City of Santa Barbara, Carlsbad, Del Mar, El Cajon, Vista and others); a current statewide list was not found. About 5.4 percent of California single family homes still report wood roofs (2020 federal survey).` },
    { id: 'zone0', grp: 'Fire code', t: 'Zone Zero: approved by the Board, not confirmed in effect', cite: 'Public Resources Code 4291 (Stats. 2025, ch. 731, AB 1455); Government Code 51182; Board of Forestry and Fire Protection, final draft approved August 19, 2026', eff: 'Statute amended October 13, 2025; regulation effective date NOT FOUND as of November 19, 2026', g: 'C', lines: ['harden', 'fire', 'repair', 'replace'], url: LAWLINK['Wildfire Task Force, Zone 0'],
      s: `The statute calls for an ember resistant zone within 5 feet of the structure, defined by Board of Forestry regulations. It does not take effect for new structures until the Board updates its regulations and guidance, and takes effect for existing structures three years after that. The Board approved the final draft on August 19, 2026. A law firm summary says the Board sent it to the Office of Administrative Law on August 28, 2026 and that approval and filing are needed before it takes effect. No source confirming that approval or an effective date was found as of November 19, 2026, so the atlas does not say the rule is in force. It will apply only in State Responsibility Areas and Very High zones in Local Responsibility Areas. Existing homes phase in: within 3 years, firewood, dead leaves, mulch and wood chips out of the first 5 feet, gutters cleaned and trees trimmed; within 5 years on a local timetable, the under eave safety zone, combustible gates, sheds and fencing. The duty that already exists: keep the roof free of leaves and needles and cut back any tree within 10 feet of a chimney outlet. CAL FIRE may not change inspections for the new zone until the State Fire Marshal finds the Legislature has funded it.` },
    { id: 'hoa', grp: 'Fire code', t: 'An association cannot require a roof that breaks the fire code', cite: 'Civil Code 4720; Health & Safety Code 13132.7(l)', eff: 'In force', g: 'B', lines: ['mf', 'replace', 'harden', 'tile'], url: LAWLINK['Civil Code 4720'],
      s: `No association may require a homeowner to install or repair a roof in a way that violates Health and Safety Code 13132.7. In a Very High zone the governing documents of a common interest development must allow at least one fire retardant roof covering material that meets the statute. A board that still specifies wood shakes, or a single product that is not Class A, can be shown the section.` },
    /* ---------- energy code ---------- */
    { id: 'coolroof', grp: 'Energy code', t: 'Cool roof rules when more than 50 percent of a roof is replaced', cite: 'Title 24, Part 6 (2025 Energy Code) 110.8(i), 150.2(b)1I, 180.2(b)1A, 141.0(b)2B, 140.3(a)1A; the 2022 code has the same values', eff: '2025 code for permit applications from January 1, 2026', g: 'A', lines: ['replace', 'tile', 'metal', 'mf', 'comm', 'coat', 'solar', 'fire'], url: LAWLINK['2025 Energy Code'],
      s: `The Energy Code has 16 climate zones, set by ZIP code. A low sloped roof has a rise to run under 2:12. Single family: when more than 50 percent of the roof is replaced, a new layer over the old one included, steep roofs in zones 4 and 8 to 15 need an aged solar reflectance of at least 0.20 and thermal emittance of 0.75, or a Solar Reflectance Index of 16; low sloped roofs in zones 4 and 6 to 15 need 0.63 and 0.75, or an index of 75. Multifamily: the trigger is more than 50 percent of the roof area or more than 2,000 square feet, whichever is less, replaced, recovered or recoated; low slope in zones 2, 4 and 6 to 15 needs 0.63 (index 75); steep in zones 4 and 8 to 15 needs 0.20 (index 16). Nonresidential, same trigger: low slope in every zone needs 0.63 (index 75); steep needs 0.20 (index 16) in zones 1 and 3 and 0.25 with emittance 0.80 (index 23) in zones 2 and 4 to 16. Hotel and motel guest rooms: low slope 0.55 (index 64) in zones 9, 10, 11, 13, 14 and 15; steep 0.20 in zones 2 to 15. Products are rated and labeled by the Cool Roof Rating Council; an unrated asphalt shingle is assumed to have an aged reflectance of 0.08 and any other unrated product 0.10.` },
    { id: 'coolex', grp: 'Energy code', t: 'Cool roof exceptions, and the insulation a low slope reroof also triggers', cite: 'Title 24, Part 6 (2025 Energy Code) exceptions to 150.2(b)1Ii and 150.2(b)1Iiia; Tables 150.2-E, 150.2-F, 141.0-B, 141.0-C; 180.2(b)1Aiii', eff: '2025 code for permit applications from January 1, 2026', g: 'A', lines: ['replace', 'tile', 'solar', 'mf', 'comm', 'coat'], url: LAWLINK['2025 Energy Code'],
      s: `Single family steep roofs are exempt where the attic is insulated to R-38 (or the ceiling U-factor is 0.025 or lower), where there is a radiant barrier in the attic not laid directly above spaced sheathing, where there are no ducts in the attic in zones 2, 4, 9, 10, 12 and 14, or where R-2 or more continuous insulation sits above or below the deck. Roof constructions weighing at least 25 pounds per square foot are exempt in every building type; that is why most concrete and clay tile reroofs are exempt (the tile inference is ours, grade D). Roof area under building integrated solar is exempt. Single family low slope roofs may trade reflectance for deck insulation, down to no reflectance requirement at R-10 in zones 6 and 7 or R-24 in zones 2, 4 and 8 to 15. A low slope reroof also triggers insulation: R-14 continuous or U-0.039 for single family and multifamily in zones 1, 2, 4 and 8 to 16 (a multifamily recoat is exempt); for nonresidential, R-23 in zones 1 to 5 and 9 to 16 and R-17 in zones 6 to 8, with at least R-10 above the deck, and a recoat is exempt. The nonresidential insulation table was read in the 2025 code only.` },
    /* ---------- solar ---------- */
    { id: 'solarnew', grp: 'Solar', t: 'Every new single family home has solar, sized by roof area', cite: 'Title 24, Part 6 (2025 Energy Code) 150.1(c)14; CPUC Decision 22-12-056 (Net Billing Tariff)', eff: 'Solar on new homes since January 1, 2020; net billing for applications from April 15, 2023', g: 'A', lines: ['newc', 'solar'], url: LAWLINK['2025 Energy Code'],
      s: `All new single family residential buildings must have a photovoltaic system. Its size is the smaller of a formula amount or the solar access roof area times 18 for steep roofs or 14 for low sloped roofs; roof areas with under 70 percent annual solar access do not count. The Energy Commission approved an exception in February 2021 for sites where snow load makes rooftop solar impractical; mountain building departments had proposed a ground snow load of 230 pounds per square foot as the limit. The Net Billing Tariff replaced net metering for customers who apply to interconnect on or after April 15, 2023. Roofers on new homes plan vents, valleys and penetrations around the array.` },
    { id: 'solarreroof', grp: 'Solar', t: 'Reroofing under existing solar: detach and reset', cite: 'Los Angeles County Building Code Manual 1512 Article 1 (April 1, 2025); City of San Diego Information Bulletin 123', eff: 'As read November 19, 2026', g: 'A', lines: ['solar', 'replace', 'tile'], url: LAWLINK['Los Angeles County reroofing manual'],
      s: `Los Angeles County requires a separate permit to reinstall a rooftop solar system removed for a reroof, on top of the building permit for the reroof. The City of San Diego requires an electrical permit to reinstall the panels. Los Angeles County states the license split: a C-39 contractor does the reroof, a C-10 electrical or C-46 solar contractor reinstalls the system, or an A or B licensee may apply for both trades. That is the County's description; a CSLB document saying the same was not opened (grade C for the CSLB position). Old bonding clips may not be reused, and standoff spacing is rechecked if the covering type changes, for example shingle to tile. Whether taking panels off and putting the same panels back changes a customer's older net metering status was NOT FOUND in the sources opened: do not state it either way without the utility's tariff text. UC fire advisers say properly installed panels do not add fire risk and that debris under and around them should be cleared.` },
    /* ---------- worker safety ---------- */
    { id: 'fall', grp: 'Safety', t: 'Fall protection at 6 feet on residential roofs', cite: 'Cal. Code Regs. tit. 8, 1731 (amended November 26, 2024); tit. 8, 1730', eff: 'July 1, 2025 (the trigger was 15 feet)', g: 'A', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['8 CCR 1731'],
      s: `On residential type roofing and reroofing, tear off included, employees on slopes up to and including 7:12 must be protected when the fall distance is 6 feet or more, by personal fall protection, scaffolding, safety nets or guardrails; a fall protection plan with safety monitors is allowed only if the employer shows the conventional methods are infeasible. On slopes steeper than 7:12 protection is required at any height. Parapets do not count toward the height. Workers get documented training on roof specific fall hazards. Other roofing falls under section 1730: protection above 20 feet, and at any height when a felt laying machine is pulled by a worker walking backwards. In 2026 Cal/OSHA cited three Los Angeles County roofing companies $282,420 after a worker repairing a leak fell about 24 feet.` },
    { id: 'heat', grp: 'Safety', t: 'Heat illness prevention: water, shade at 80 degrees, high heat steps at 95', cite: 'Cal. Code Regs. tit. 8, 3395', eff: 'In force (first adopted August 22, 2005)', g: 'A', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['8 CCR 3395'],
      s: `Outdoor employers provide one quart of drinking water per employee per hour for the whole shift, and shade when the temperature exceeds 80 degrees Fahrenheit. At 95 degrees or more, employers in listed industries, construction among them, apply high heat procedures. Inland summer production is planned around these limits.` },
    { id: 'asbestos', grp: 'Safety', t: 'Asbestos roofing and lead: the 100 square foot line', cite: 'Cal. Code Regs. tit. 8, 1529, 341.6, 1532.1; Bus. & Prof. Code 7058.5, 7058.6; California Residential Code R908.3.1.1', eff: 'In force; lead limits from January 1, 2025', g: 'A', lines: ['replace', 'repair', 'tile', 'mf', 'comm', 'fire'], url: LAWLINK['8 CCR 1529'],
      s: `Removing asbestos containing roofing is Class II asbestos work. An employer disturbing 100 square feet or more of asbestos containing material must register with Cal/OSHA first, and the contractor needs the C-22 class or the board's asbestos certification; an uncertified contractor may still take the job if a C-22 or certified contractor does the asbestos part. Asbestos cement tile may not be covered over with a new layer. For lead in construction the permissible exposure limit is 10 micrograms per cubic meter and the action level is 2, from January 1, 2025. The federal lead renovation rule and the air district asbestos notification rules were not opened for this build (not verified).` },
    /* ---------- defects ---------- */
    { id: 'defect', grp: 'Defects', t: 'Roof defect claims: four years if visible, ten if hidden', cite: 'Code Civ. Proc. 337.1, 337.15; Bus. & Prof. Code 7159(e)(5)', eff: 'In force', g: 'A', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['Code Civ. Proc. 337.15'],
      s: `No action for a patent deficiency may be brought more than four years after substantial completion, and none for a latent deficiency, one not apparent by reasonable inspection, more than 10 years after. The ordinary limitation periods that run inside those outer limits (Code of Civil Procedure 337, 338 and 339) were not read for this build. The notice every home improvement contract carries tells owners that the CSLB can investigate a complaint filed within the legal deadline, usually four years.` },
    { id: 'sb800', grp: 'Defects', t: 'Right to Repair Act: the roof standards for new homes', cite: 'Civil Code 896(a)(4), (g)(3)(A), (g)(11), 900, 910, 938, 941 (SB 800)', eff: 'New homes sold on or after January 1, 2003', g: 'A', lines: ['newc'], url: LAWLINK['Civil Code 896'],
      s: `The Act covers original construction sold as individual dwelling units; it does not govern an ordinary reroof contract (our reading). The builder, and to the extent the Act sets out a subcontractor such as the roofer, is liable under its standards: roofs, roofing systems, chimney caps and ventilation components must not let water into the structure or past the moisture barriers; roofing must be installed so that material does not fall from the roof; and manufactured products, roofs included, must be installed so as not to shorten their useful life. No shorter period than the general 10 years is stated for the two roof standards. The builder gives a one year fit and finish warranty, and an owner must give written notice and a chance to repair before suing.` },
    /* ---------- programs ---------- */
    { id: 'ladwp', grp: 'Programs', t: 'Cool roof rebate: Los Angeles Department of Water and Power', cite: 'LADWP Consumer Rebate Program fact sheet (dated April 2024, posted February 2026)', eff: 'As posted February 2026', g: 'A', lines: ['replace', 'coat', 'tile', 'metal'], url: LAWLINK['LADWP rebate fact sheet'],
      s: `The utility's fact sheet lists a cool roof rebate of up to $0.60 per square foot for its own customers. The tier rules were not read. Rebate amounts for other utilities and cities (SMUD, Pasadena and others) were not found, and PACE financing was not researched. Quote a rebate only for the utility that publishes it, with its date.` },
    /* ---------- advertising and selling: federal rules carried over, and what was not read ---------- */
    { id: 'cooling', grp: 'Federal', t: 'Federal rule: three days to cancel a sale made at the home', cite: '16 CFR Part 429 (FTC Cooling Off Rule)', eff: 'In force', g: 'A', lines: LX_RES, url: LAWLINK['16 CFR 429'],
      s: `A sale of $25 or more made at the buyer's home carries a right to cancel within three business days, with the written notice the rule prescribes. An emergency exception exists only where the buyer starts the contact, needs the work for a genuine emergency and waives the right in a separate signed statement; canvassing after a fire or a storm does not fit it. California's own notice gives more: five business days for a senior citizen and seven for disaster repairs (see the right to cancel row). Read on ecfr.gov on October 6, 2026.` },
    { id: 'reviews', grp: 'Federal', t: 'Reviews and testimonials', cite: '16 CFR Part 465 (eff. October 21, 2024); 16 CFR Part 255', eff: 'In force', g: 'A', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['16 CFR 465'],
      s: `No fake or AI written reviews, no reviews bought on the condition that they are positive, no undisclosed reviews by staff or relatives, no company controlled "independent" review sites, no suppression of negative reviews, and no fake social media indicators. Civil penalties apply per violation. Read on ecfr.gov on October 6, 2026.` },
    { id: 'regz', grp: 'Federal', t: 'Financing ads and trigger terms', cite: '12 CFR 1026.24 (Regulation Z)', eff: 'In force', g: 'A', lines: ['replace', 'metal', 'tile', 'harden', 'solar', 'mh', 'repair'], url: LAWLINK['12 CFR 1026.24'],
      s: `A financing ad that states a payment amount, a number or period of payments, a down payment or a finance charge must also state the down payment, the terms of repayment and the annual percentage rate, using that term. "Financing available" alone triggers nothing. In California the down payment itself is capped (see the down payment row), and a home improvement contract shows any finance charge separately. Read on ecfr.gov on October 6, 2026.` },
    { id: 'tcpa', grp: 'Federal', t: 'Calls and texts', cite: '47 U.S.C. 227; 47 CFR 64.1200', eff: 'In force', g: 'B', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['47 CFR 64.1200'],
      s: `Autodialed or prerecorded marketing calls and texts to mobile phones need prior express written consent, and marketing calls must honor the national Do Not Call list. Lead lists bought from third parties after a fire or a storm rarely carry valid consent. Any California rule on telephone solicitation was not read for this build.` },
    { id: 'truth', grp: 'Advertising', t: 'Truthful advertising: what was read and what was not', cite: 'Penal Code 396(h); Civil Code 1797.93; Cal. Code Regs. tit. 10, 2644.9; FTC Act section 5', eff: 'In force', g: 'D', lines: LX_ALL, url: LAWLINK['Civil Code 1797.93'],
      s: `California's general unfair competition and false advertising statutes were not read for this build, so the register cites no section for them. What was read: a price gouging violation is also unfair competition (Penal Code 396); a "lifetime" roof warranty must say whose or what lifetime (Civil Code 1797.93); the Safer from Wildfires regulation sets no discount percentage; and Class A is the rating of a tested assembly. On the fire evidence, about 90 percent of structures inside the January 2025 Palisades and Eaton perimeters had Class A coverings by CAL FIRE's inspection data, and IBHS found those roofs were often paired with vulnerable vents, eaves, windows and doors. Copy must therefore never say "fireproof", promise a discount size or promise that an insurer will renew. Superlatives need a dated, verifiable basis.` },
  ],
  pending: [
    { t: 'Zone Zero: approved August 19, 2026, effective date not confirmed', s: 'The Board of Forestry and Fire Protection approved the final draft of the Zone 0 defensible space regulations on August 19, 2026. A law firm alert of September 9, 2026 says the Board sent them to the Office of Administrative Law on August 28, 2026. No source confirming that Office\'s approval, the final text or an effective date was found as of November 19, 2026. An August 2026 trade press report said new construction would be covered from September; that was not confirmed. The Board cut the fully noncombustible strip to a minimum of one foot. Do not write that Zone Zero is in force, and do not sell gutter or eave work as required by it, until the filed rule is read.', g: 'C', src: 'California Wildfire and Forest Resilience Task Force, August 2026; Best Best & Krieger alert, September 9, 2026; Insurance Journal, August 20, 2026' },
    { t: 'California Safe Homes grants: funded, amounts not set', s: 'Assembly Bill 888 of 2025 took effect January 1, 2026. The Department of Insurance is to run grants for low and middle income owners in high risk areas insured by an admitted insurer or the FAIR Plan, toward fire safe roofs and the five foot zone. A news report said $3 million had been secured to start and that grant amounts had not been determined. Whether applications opened in 2026 was not found. Copy must not promise a grant or state an amount.', g: 'C', src: 'CalMatters, January 1, 2026, quoting the Department of Insurance' },
    { t: 'California Wildfire Mitigation Program: amounts not found', s: 'Per home amounts and homes completed under the state home hardening retrofit program were not found in any primary source; only aggregator pages with conflicting amounts appeared. The atlas states none.', g: 'D', src: 'Search of November 19, 2026' },
    { t: 'Public adjuster fee cap did not pass, as far as seen', s: 'Assembly Bill 597 of 2025 would have capped public adjuster fees at 15 percent. Bill trackers seen only in a search summary show it held in committee on August 29, 2025. No sign that it became law was found.', g: 'D', src: 'Search summary of bill tracking sites' },
    { t: 'Workers\' compensation for every licensee: now due January 1, 2028', s: 'Senate Bill 216 of 2022 planned to require workers\' compensation of all contractors, with or without employees, by January 1, 2026. The current section 7125 instead sunsets on January 1, 2028, which is when the wider rule turns on. Roofers already carry it; from 2028 the competitors who claim no employees must too. The board must report to the Legislature by 2027 on a new process to verify exemptions (SB 291).', g: 'B', src: 'Bus. & Prof. Code 7125 (california.public.law, updated January 1, 2025); CSLB press release 22-08; CSLB Industry Bulletin 25-04' },
    { t: 'Residential building standards frozen until June 1, 2031', s: 'Assembly Bill 130 of 2025 stops new state and local residential building standards from October 1, 2025 to June 1, 2031, except emergency standards and home hardening. Expect the reroof, cool roof and solar rules in this register to hold, and expect cities to keep amending the wildfire code.', g: 'A', src: 'California Building Standards Commission, Information Bulletin 25-03, September 22, 2025' },
    { t: 'Net metering after a detach and reset: not found', s: 'No source opened says whether removing rooftop panels for a reroof and putting the same panels back changes a customer\'s older net metering status. Until the utility tariff text is read, a solar detach and reset page must not say that the status is safe or that it is lost.', g: 'D', src: 'CPUC Net Billing Tariff page (read); utility tariffs (not read)' },
    { t: 'Fire zone maps and local shake bans: check the address', s: 'The 2025 Fire Hazard Severity Zone maps were seen only through city pages in search summaries, and the only current local wood shake rules confirmed are Los Angeles and San Diego. A statewide list of local bans was not found. Screen each address with the State Fire Marshal\'s viewer and the city building department.', g: 'D', src: 'Search summaries; Los Angeles and San Diego bulletins' },
  ],
};
